      "id": "j401-h2-anchors",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "## 1. Cell Anchors — Naming Your Cells\n\nAdd `//@ anchor: name` as the first line of any code cell to give it a **stable, semantic name**.\nAnchors make cells reusable and referenceable by other cells and pipelines.\n\n```\n//@ anchor: math-utils\n//@ description: Core math functions\n```\n\nThe buttons **▶ With Deps** and **Run Pipeline** use these names to build the execution graph.",
      "output": "",
      "returnValue": null,
      "executed": false,
      "executionCount": null,
      "error": "",
      "anchor": null,
      "dependsOn": [],
      "pipelineSteps": []
    },
    {
      "id": "j401-imports",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "//@ anchor: imports\n//@ description: Common imports for the 401 notebook\n// One cell that every other cell can declare a dependency on. Keeping the\n// imports here means the later cells stay about their own subject, and the\n// dependency graph has a single clear root.\nimport java.util.*;          // List, Map, ArrayList, Comparator, ...\nimport java.util.stream.*;   // Stream, IntStream, Collectors\nimport java.util.function.*; // Function, Predicate, Supplier\nimport java.time.*;          // LocalDate, used by the transaction records below\n\n// The bracketed prefix is a habit worth copying in a notebook: when several\n// cells have run into the same session, it tells you which one produced a line.\nSystem.out.println(\"[imports] Ready\");",
      "output": "[imports] Ready\r\n",
      "returnValue": "",
      "executed": true,
      "executionCount": 1,
      "error": "",
      "anchor": "imports",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "pipelineSteps": []
    },
    {
      "id": "j401-math-utils",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "//@ anchor: math-utils\n//@ namespace: barista.utils.math\n//@ depends: imports\n//@ description: Reusable math utilities — prime checking, statistics, fibonacci\n\n// 'depends: imports' means Arima runs the imports cell first if it has not\n// already run in this session, so these methods can rely on java.util.stream\n// being in scope without importing it again.\n\nboolean isPrime(int n) {\n    if (n < 2) return false;\n    // i * i <= n is the same test as i <= sqrt(n), but stays in integer\n    // arithmetic - no square root per iteration, no floating-point rounding.\n    for (int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) if (n % i == 0) return false;\n    return true;\n}\n\nList<Integer> primesUpTo(int max) {\n    // rangeClosed includes the upper bound; range() would stop one short.\n    // boxed() converts IntStream (primitive ints) into Stream<Integer>, which\n    // is what a List can actually hold.\n    return IntStream.rangeClosed(2, max).filter(n -> isPrime(n))\n        .boxed().collect(Collectors.toList());\n}\n\ndouble mean(List<Integer> nums) {\n    // average() returns OptionalDouble because an empty list has no mean.\n    // orElse(0) picks the fallback here rather than leaving the caller to.\n    return nums.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).average().orElse(0);\n}\n\ndouble stdDev(List<Integer> nums) {\n    double m = mean(nums);\n    // Population standard deviation: the root of the mean squared deviation.\n    // (A sample standard deviation would divide by n-1 instead of n.)\n    return Math.sqrt(nums.stream().mapToDouble(n -> (n-m)*(n-m)).average().orElse(0));\n}\n\nlong fib(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return n;\n    // Iterative, not recursive: naive recursion recomputes the same values\n    // exponentially many times, while this walks the sequence once.\n    long a = 0, b = 1;\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) { long t = a+b; a = b; b = t; }\n    return b;\n}\n\nSystem.out.println(\"[math-utils] isPrime(17)=\" + isPrime(17) + \" mean(1..5)=\" + mean(List.of(1,2,3,4,5)));",
      "output": "[math-utils] isPrime(17)=true mean(1..5)=3.0\r\n",
      "returnValue": "",
      "executed": true,
      "executionCount": 2,
      "error": "",
      "anchor": "math-utils",
      "dependsOn": [
        "imports"
      ],
      "pipelineSteps": []
    },
    {
      "id": "j401-string-utils",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "//@ anchor: string-utils\n//@ namespace: barista.utils.strings\n//@ depends: imports\n//@ description: String manipulation utilities\n\nString camelToSnake(String s) {\n    // The regex captures a lowercase letter followed by an uppercase one, and\n    // \"$1_$2\" puts an underscore between the two captured groups. Matching the\n    // BOUNDARY rather than the uppercase letter alone is what stops a leading\n    // capital from producing a stray underscore at the front.\n    return s.replaceAll(\"([a-z])([A-Z])\", \"$1_$2\").toLowerCase();\n}\n\nString capitalize(String s) {\n    // Guard first: charAt(0) on an empty String would throw, and null would\n    // throw before that.\n    if (s == null || s.isEmpty()) return s;\n    // Character.toUpperCase returns a char, so the + here is char + String,\n    // which Java resolves as concatenation.\n    return Character.toUpperCase(s.charAt(0)) + s.substring(1).toLowerCase();\n}\n\nList<String> tokenize(String text) {\n    // The split pattern is a CHARACTER CLASS plus '+': one or more of\n    // whitespace, comma, semicolon, dot, bang or question mark, treated as a\n    // single separator. Without the '+', \"a, b\" would yield an empty token.\n    return Arrays.stream(text.split(\"[\\\\s,;.!?]+\"))\n        .filter(w -> !w.isBlank()).map(String::toLowerCase)\n        .collect(Collectors.toList());\n}\n\nMap<String, Long> wordFrequency(String text) {\n    // groupingBy with counting() is the idiomatic word count: the classifier\n    // w -> w makes each distinct word its own key, and counting() replaces the\n    // group's list with its size. Long, not Integer, because counting() counts\n    // without any assumption about how large the result can get.\n    return tokenize(text).stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(w -> w, Collectors.counting()));\n}\n\nSystem.out.println(\"[string-utils] camelToSnake='\" + camelToSnake(\"myVariableName\") + \"'\");",
      "output": "[string-utils] camelToSnake='my_variable_name'\r\n",
      "returnValue": "",
      "executed": true,
      "executionCount": 3,
      "error": "",
      "anchor": "string-utils",
      "dependsOn": [
        "imports"
      ],
      "pipelineSteps": []
    },
