      "id": "j401-h2-pipeline",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "## 5. Pipeline Orchestration — Chaining Steps\n\nA **PIPELINE cell** defines an ordered workflow of named steps. It's like an Ant build target\nthat chains other targets. Click **Run Pipeline** on the PIPELINE cell below.\n\n```\n//@ steps: step-a, step-b, step-c\n```\n\nArima:\n1. Resolves each step's full transitive dependency closure\n2. Merges and deduplicates the graph\n3. Topologically sorts everything\n4. Executes once in the correct order — no duplicate runs\n\n**Use case:** ETL pipeline, analysis workflow, test suite.",
      "output": "",
      "returnValue": null,
      "executed": false,
      "executionCount": null,
      "error": "",
      "anchor": null,
      "dependsOn": [],
      "pipelineSteps": []
    },
    {
      "id": "j401-data-load",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "//@ anchor: data-load\n//@ depends: imports\n//@ description: Load and validate raw transaction data\n\n// Step 1 of the pipeline. Each ETL stage is its own anchored cell so that the\n// stages can be re-run, reordered and depended on individually.\n\n// A record is the right shape for a data row: named fields, value equality,\n// and immutability, so a later stage cannot quietly rewrite the input.\nrecord Transaction(String id, String category, double amount, LocalDate date) {}\n\n// List.of is immutable, which is what you want for a fixed input set.\n// LocalDate.of takes a real month number (1 = January) - unlike the old\n// java.util.Date, which was zero-based and a well-known source of off-by-one\n// bugs. (double for money is fine for a demo; real ledgers use BigDecimal,\n// because 0.1 has no exact binary representation.)\nvar rawData = List.of(\n    new Transaction(\"T001\", \"Food\",      12.50, LocalDate.of(2026, 1, 5)),\n    new Transaction(\"T002\", \"Transport\",  8.00, LocalDate.of(2026, 1, 6)),\n    new Transaction(\"T003\", \"Food\",      45.00, LocalDate.of(2026, 1, 7)),\n    new Transaction(\"T004\", \"Utilities\", 89.50, LocalDate.of(2026, 1, 8)),\n    new Transaction(\"T005\", \"Food\",      22.30, LocalDate.of(2026, 1, 9)),\n    new Transaction(\"T006\", \"Transport\", 15.00, LocalDate.of(2026, 1,10)),\n    new Transaction(\"T007\", \"Shopping\", 130.00, LocalDate.of(2026, 1,11)),\n    new Transaction(\"T008\", \"Utilities\",  45.0, LocalDate.of(2026, 1,12))\n);\n\nSystem.out.println(\"[data-load] Loaded \" + rawData.size() + \" transactions\");\n// %6.2f reserves six columns so the amounts line up on the decimal point.\nrawData.forEach(t -> System.out.printf(\"  %s %-12s $%6.2f  %s%n\",\n    t.id(), t.category(), t.amount(), t.date()));",
      "output": "[data-load] Loaded 8 transactions\r\n  T001 Food         $ 12.50  2026-01-05\r\n  T002 Transport    $  8.00  2026-01-06\r\n  T003 Food         $ 45.00  2026-01-07\r\n  T004 Utilities    $ 89.50  2026-01-08\r\n  T005 Food         $ 22.30  2026-01-09\r\n  T006 Transport    $ 15.00  2026-01-10\r\n  T007 Shopping     $130.00  2026-01-11\r\n  T008 Utilities    $ 45.00  2026-01-12\r\n",
      "returnValue": "",
      "executed": true,
      "executionCount": 8,
      "error": "",
      "anchor": "data-load",
      "dependsOn": [
        "imports"
      ],
      "pipelineSteps": []
    },
    {
      "id": "j401-data-transform",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "//@ anchor: data-transform\n//@ depends: data-load\n//@ description: Enrich and transform transaction data\n\n// Step 2. Enrichment adds derived fields WITHOUT touching the originals: each\n// EnrichedTx wraps the original Transaction rather than copying its fields, so\n// the raw data stays available and nothing can drift out of sync.\nrecord EnrichedTx(Transaction tx, String quarter, boolean isLargeExpense) {}\n\nvar enriched = rawData.stream().map(t -> new EnrichedTx(\n    t,\n    // Months are 1..12; subtracting 1 makes them 0..11 so that integer\n    // division by 3 buckets them evenly, and adding 1 puts them back into\n    // Q1..Q4. Without the -1, March and April would land in the same quarter.\n    \"Q\" + ((t.date().getMonthValue() - 1) / 3 + 1),\n    t.amount() > 50.0   // the business rule, stated once and stored\n)).collect(Collectors.toList());\n\nSystem.out.println(\"[data-transform] Enriched \" + enriched.size() + \" transactions\");\nenriched.stream().filter(e -> e.isLargeExpense())\n    .forEach(e -> System.out.printf(\"  Large: %s $%.2f (%s)%n\",\n        e.tx().category(), e.tx().amount(), e.quarter()));",
      "output": "[data-transform] Enriched 8 transactions\r\n  Large: Utilities $89.50 (Q1)\r\n  Large: Shopping $130.00 (Q1)\r\n",
      "returnValue": "",
      "executed": true,
      "executionCount": 9,
      "error": "",
      "anchor": "data-transform",
      "dependsOn": [
        "data-load"
      ],
      "pipelineSteps": []
    },
    {
      "id": "j401-data-aggregate",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "//@ anchor: data-aggregate\n//@ depends: data-transform\n//@ description: Aggregate enriched data by category\n\n// Step 3. summarizingDouble collects count, sum, min, max and average in ONE\n// pass and returns a DoubleSummaryStatistics per group - far better than\n// streaming the same data five times to answer five questions.\nvar byCategory = enriched.stream().collect(\n    Collectors.groupingBy(\n        e -> e.tx().category(),                              // the key\n        Collectors.summarizingDouble(e -> e.tx().amount())   // what to do per group\n    )\n);\n\nSystem.out.println(\"[data-aggregate] Spending by category:\");\nbyCategory.entrySet().stream()\n    // Sorting by a field INSIDE the value: comparingByValue accepts a\n    // comparator for the values themselves, so this reads as \"compare entries\n    // by their statistics' total, then flip to descending\".\n    .sorted(Map.Entry.<String, DoubleSummaryStatistics>comparingByValue(\n        Comparator.comparingDouble(DoubleSummaryStatistics::getSum)).reversed())\n    .forEach(e -> System.out.printf(\"  %-12s count=%-2d total=$%.2f  avg=$%.2f%n\",\n        // getCount() is a long; %d accepts it, and the cast to int is here only\n        // to keep the intent of a small count explicit.\n        e.getKey(), (int)e.getValue().getCount(),\n        e.getValue().getSum(), e.getValue().getAverage()));",
      "output": "[data-aggregate] Spending by category:\r\n  Utilities    count=2  total=$134.50  avg=$67.25\r\n  Shopping     count=1  total=$130.00  avg=$130.00\r\n  Food         count=3  total=$79.80  avg=$26.60\r\n  Transport    count=2  total=$23.00  avg=$11.50\r\n",
      "returnValue": "",
      "executed": true,
      "executionCount": 10,
      "error": "",
      "anchor": "data-aggregate",
      "dependsOn": [
        "data-transform"
      ],
      "pipelineSteps": []
    },
    {
      "id": "j401-data-report",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "//@ anchor: data-report\n//@ depends: data-aggregate, math-utils\n//@ description: Generate a final summary report\n\n// Step 4, and the one that shows why dependencies are declared rather than\n// implied by cell order: this cell needs the aggregation AND the math-utils\n// helpers, which live in a different branch of the graph entirely.\n\ndouble totalSpend = byCategory.values().stream()\n    .mapToDouble(DoubleSummaryStatistics::getSum).sum();\n\nSystem.out.println(\"\\n=== MONTHLY SPEND REPORT ===\");\nSystem.out.printf(\"Total transactions : %d%n\", rawData.size());\nSystem.out.printf(\"Total spend        : $%.2f%n\", totalSpend);\nSystem.out.printf(\"Average per tx     : $%.2f%n\", totalSpend / rawData.size());\n\n// max() gives an Optional, so the whole chain ends with orElse rather than a\n// null check - and formatting only happens if there was a maximum to format.\nSystem.out.println(\"\\nTop category: \" + byCategory.entrySet().stream()\n    .max(Comparator.comparingDouble(e -> e.getValue().getSum()))\n    .map(e -> e.getKey() + \" ($\" + String.format(\"%.2f\", e.getValue().getSum()) + \")\")\n    .orElse(\"none\"));\n\n// stdDev() from math-utils takes List<Integer>, so the amounts are converted\n// to whole CENTS first. Scaling by 100 before truncating keeps the precision\n// that a straight (int) cast on dollars would have thrown away; dividing the\n// result by 100 afterwards puts the answer back into dollars.\nvar amounts = rawData.stream().map(t -> (int)(t.amount() * 100))\n    .collect(Collectors.toList());\nSystem.out.printf(\"Amount std-dev     : $%.2f%n\", stdDev(amounts) / 100.0);",
      "output": "\n=== MONTHLY SPEND REPORT ===\r\nTotal transactions : 8\r\nTotal spend        : $367.30\r\nAverage per tx     : $45.91\r\n\nTop category: Utilities ($134.50)\r\nAmount std-dev     : $40.37\r\n",
      "returnValue": "java.io.PrintStream@28c97a5",
      "executed": true,
      "executionCount": 11,
      "error": "",
      "anchor": "data-report",
      "dependsOn": [
        "data-aggregate",
        "math-utils"
      ],
      "pipelineSteps": []
    },
