    {
      "id": "j401-mcp-execute",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "//@ anchor: mcp-execute\n//@ depends: mcp-demo\n//@ description: Execute code via MCP tool call\n\n// tools/list discovers what is available; tools/call actually invokes one.\n// This cell calls barista_execute_code - so JShell is asking Arima to run\n// Java for it, which is the same round trip an AI agent would make.\nimport java.net.http.*;\nimport java.net.URI;\n\nvar client = HttpClient.newHttpClient();\n\n// The nesting here is worth reading slowly: params.name selects the tool, and\n// params.arguments is the tool's OWN payload. The \\\\\\\" sequences are quotes\n// that survive two levels of escaping - Java string, then JSON string - because\n// the Java snippet being sent contains quotes of its own.\n// 'session' names the JShell session on the server side, so an agent can keep\n// state across several calls exactly as a notebook does across cells.\nvar toolCallBody = \"{\\\"jsonrpc\\\":\\\"2.0\\\",\\\"id\\\":2,\\\"method\\\":\\\"tools/call\\\",\" +\n    \"\\\"params\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"barista_execute_code\\\",\" +\n    \"\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"code\\\":\\\"var result = IntStream.rangeClosed(1,10).sum(); System.out.println(result);\\\",\\\"session\\\":\\\"mcp-agent-session\\\"}}}\";\n\n\nvar request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()\n    .uri(URI.create(\"http://localhost:8585/api/mcp/messages\"))\n    .header(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\")\n    .POST(HttpRequest.BodyPublishers.ofString(toolCallBody))\n    .build();\n\nvar response = client.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());\nSystem.out.println(\"MCP tool call result:\");\n\n// An MCP result carries a content array of typed blocks; the interesting one\n// here is the \"text\" block holding whatever the code printed.\nvar body = response.body();\nint textIdx = body.indexOf(\"\\\"text\\\":\");\nif (textIdx >= 0) {\n    int start = body.indexOf(\"\\\"\", textIdx + 7) + 1;\n    // lastIndexOf, not indexOf: the text block runs to the LAST quote in the\n    // payload. Another reminder that this is scanning, not parsing.\n    int end = body.lastIndexOf(\"\\\"\");\n    if (start > 0 && end > start)\n        // The server sent real newlines as the two characters \\ and n inside\n        // JSON; turning them back into line breaks is what makes the output\n        // print on separate lines.\n        System.out.println(\"  Output: \" + body.substring(start, end).replace(\"\\\\n\", \"\\n\"));\n}",
      "output": "",
      "returnValue": null,
      "executed": false,
      "executionCount": 19,
      "error": "",
      "anchor": "mcp-execute",
      "dependsOn": [
        "mcp-demo"
      ],
      "pipelineSteps": []
    },
    {
      "id": "j401-footer",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "---\n\n## Summary\n\nYou've learned Arima Notebooks' full orchestration system:\n\n| Concept | Syntax | Purpose |\n|---------|--------|---------|\n| **Anchor** | `//@ anchor: name` | Name a cell for reuse |\n| **Depends** | `//@ depends: a, b` | Declare prerequisites |\n| **Namespace** | `//@ namespace: com.x.y` | Package scope for exports |\n| **Cross-notebook** | `//@ depends: notebook:id/anchor` | Import from any notebook |\n| **Pipeline** | `//@ steps: a, b, c` | Ordered workflow |\n| **MCP** | `GET /api/mcp/sse` | AI agent tool interface |\n\n### Quick Reference Card:\n```java\n//@ anchor: my-utils          ← names this cell\n//@ namespace: com.mine.utils  ← wraps as VNS_com_mine_utils when imported\n//@ depends: imports, base     ← runs these first (same notebook)\n//@ depends: notebook:java-101/methods  ← imports from another notebook\n//@ steps: load, transform, report      ← (PIPELINE cell only)\n//@ description: What this cell does    ← documentation\n//@ on-error: continue                  ← pipeline error handling (stop/continue)\n```",
